.TH CMCLDEPS 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
cmcldeps \- run rc and record the headers a resource script includes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cmcldeps RC
.I source depfile object prefix cl rc
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cmcldeps
is an internal helper of
.BR cmake (1).
When CMake's Ninja generator compiles a resource script with Microsoft
.B rc
on a Windows host, as it does for MSVC and compilers that imitate it, the
build rule runs the
.B rc
command through
.B cmcldeps
so that Ninja learns which headers the script includes.
.B rc
cannot list them, so
.B cmcldeps
preprocesses the script with the MSVC compiler
.I cl
to find them, in three steps:
.IP 1. 4
Run
.I cl
with
.B "/P /DRC_INVOKED /nologo /showIncludes /TC"
followed by the
.I argument
list, in the directory part of
.IR object ,
up to its last backslash, or in the current directory if it has none.
In this list, every occurrence of
.BR /fo ,
.BR \-fo ,
.BR /nologo ,
or
.B \-nologo
followed by a space is removed, and every occurrence of the text of
.I object
becomes
.BI \-Fo object .obj\fR.
.I cl
writes no object, but leaves the preprocessed script, a
.B .i
file named after the script, in that directory.
.IP 2. 4
If
.I cl
fails, exit with its status.
Otherwise write
.IR depfile ,
described below.
.IP 3. 4
Run
.I rc
with the
.I argument
list in the current directory, and exit with its status.
If the list contained
.B /nologo
or
.B \-nologo
followed by a space,
.I rc
gets a single
.B /nologo
before it.
.PP
The standard output and standard error of each tool are captured together.
Lines that start with
.I prefix
name included files and are dropped, as is the first other line that
starts with the part of
.I source
after its last backslash, which
.I cl
prints first.
This is the only use of
.IR source .
When the tool fails, the remaining lines are written to standard output.
When it succeeds they are discarded, so warnings never appear.
.PP
.I prefix
is the localized text that
.B /showIncludes
puts before each included file, such as
.B "Note: including file:"
in English.
CMake passes the value of
.BR CMAKE_CL_SHOWINCLUDES_PREFIX ,
which it detects when it tests the compiler, and which is empty if that
detection fails.
An empty
.I prefix
matches every line, so every line counts as an included file and nothing
is printed, even when the tool fails.
.SS Command line
.B cmcldeps
splits its command line itself, not by the C runtime rules.
Arguments are separated by spaces.
Double quotes start and end runs in which spaces do not separate, and are
removed only when they enclose the whole argument.
Backslashes are not special.
Every argument up to and including
.I rc
must be followed by a space.
The rest of the line is the
.I argument
list, which is split by the Microsoft C runtime rules when a tool runs.
.PP
The first argument must be
.BR RC .
The usage message also names
.B C
and
.BR CXX ,
but they are rejected.
Errors are reported on standard error as
.B "ninja: FATAL:"
followed by the reason and a usage summary.
.SS Dependency file
.I depfile
is written only when
.I cl
succeeds, and replaces any existing file.
An empty
.I depfile
argument writes none.
If it cannot be created,
.B cmcldeps
carries on without it.
It is one Make rule with
.I object
as the target and each included file on its own line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
CMakeFiles\e\eapp.dir\e\eapp.rc.res: \e
C:\e\esdk\e\eum\e\ewinres.h \e
gen\e\eversion.h \e
C:\e\esrc\e\emy\e app\e\eresource.h \e
.EE
.RE
.PP
An empty line ends the file, and lines end in LF.
Each file name is the rest of its line after
.IR prefix ,
without leading spaces or a final carriage return.
The names are sorted and exact duplicates dropped, then forward slashes
become backslashes, and the current directory is removed from the start
of a name, matching case.
Each backslash is doubled and each space escaped with a backslash.
.I source
itself is not listed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for
.I cl
and
.I rc
when they are named without a directory.
The current directory is not searched.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Each tool succeeded or could not be started.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line.
.TP
.I n
.I cl
or
.I rc
failed with status
.IR n .
.SH EXAMPLES
List the commands in a CMake build directory that use
.BR cmcldeps :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ninja \-t commands | grep cmcldeps
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compile
.I app.rc
to
.I app.res
with the
.B cl
and
.B rc
found on
.BR PATH ,
writing the headers it includes to
.IR app.res.d :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cmcldeps RC app.rc app.res.d app.res "Note: including file:" \e
    cl rc /fo app.res app.rc
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
With the kit's own GCC toolchain, CMake compiles resource scripts with
.BR windres (1)
and never runs
.BR cmcldeps .
It runs only when the kit's
.B cmake
uses the Ninja generator with MSVC or a compatible compiler, and the kit's
.B cmake
picks Ninja by default even when Visual Studio is installed.
Running it by hand is rarely useful, except to repeat a failing build step.
.PP
If
.I cl
or
.I rc
cannot be started, for example because it is not found,
.B cmcldeps
prints nothing and treats the tool as successful.
The same happens to
.I cl
when the directory of
.I object
does not exist.
Without
.IR cl ,
it writes no
.I depfile
but still runs
.IR rc .
Without
.IR rc ,
it exits with status 0 although nothing wrote
.IR object .
.PP
Because
.I cl
runs in the directory of
.I object
and
.I rc
in the current directory, a relative path in the
.I argument
list means different files to the two tools, and a relative
.I cl
path is resolved from the directory of
.IR object .
.PP
Tool output lines that end in CR LF are written with an extra CR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cmake (1),
.BR windres (1),
.BR cmake\-generators (7)
